  Ok, I'm a steak and potatoes kinda guy. I don't like most salad dressings,
and I hate the taste of Brocoli. (however, I like just about every other
vegetable). My usual meal consists of steak+green vegetable+potatoes,
baked ham+cabbage/string beans+potatoes/turnips, fried or baked chicken
with stuffing and corn/peas, beef stew, spaghetti/lasagne, bean soup,
chicken noodle soup(usually made with leftovers of baked chicken), fish
and french fries, etc. Being a stereotypical hacker, I consume large
quantities of pizza and Coke also.

  The closest (in terms of tastyness)veget meal I've had compared to
a meat diet was chow mein without the meat.  I remain skeptical 
that a veget diet could be as filling/tasty as a meaty one. I think
that animal fat may contribute some way to the taste that I'm not able
to pin down (supermarket diet meals always taste terrible. There is a book
out called "The fat tooth" that posits that some people have an affinity for
fat like sugar.)  My skepticism is probably related to my lack of
real world experience -- I can't imagine a veget meal that has the
spicy/juicy kick to it that a baked chicken/broiled steak has.  Someone
mentioned that salt might be the key, and they may be right. Chow mein
tastes good to me because I add liberal doses of soy sauce which has
lots of salt in it. BTW, is anyone else attracted to the taste of
browned meat/fat? I find that broiled meat/fat has an alkaline-like taste to it
in the burnt parts (carbon deposits?). I love over-welldone food.
("give me one steak, Nuke it please.")

  I've been raised since birth on a standard middle American diet and
McDonalds.  I might be psychologically addicted to the taste of meat the
way a drug addict is attracted to heroin.  I may be able to reduce the
amount of animal products in my diet, but I doubt I'll be able to eliminate
them the way Perry has.  Maybe NLP will be able to help me deprogram
this primal affinities one day, but for now: where is that quarter pounder
with cheese?

-Ray, Herbivore in training.
  

