From: szabo@techbook.com (Nick Szabo)
Subject: DIET: Frying, nutrition & polyphenols

Perry Metzger:
> Myself, I avoid fried foods -- I try to consume as little fat as
> possible.

Dittos.  Frying in particular cross-links and chars oils, proteins,
etc. creating a whole potpourri of carcinogens.  I favor the microwave 
oven and boiled or raw low-fat foods.  I do have some concerns about
the effects of very hot food on the esophagus, though, since I
sometimes find myself wolfing down quick microwaved meals.

On the original question, I greatly reduce proteins and 
fat in general, but I don't completely eschew meat, and I don't
try to substitute vegetable proteins for meat.  I figure to
minimize total amino acid intake I want to minimize excess 
amino acids, and that means avoiding unbalanced protein sources
in dairy, legumes, etc. and getting balanced protein with
low-fat boiled meat, eg in soups.  

I echo Stanton in emphasizing the importance of megavitamin
and mineral supplements, especially  E, C, beta-carotene, zinc, 
chromium, and selenium.

What do y'all think about the new polyphenol supplements?  
I've seen several scientific papers on antioxidants studying
various extracts from green tea, wine, etc. with some good
results at the cellular level (eg more effective then E or C at
stopping ischemic reperfusion injury).  TwinLabs has a new Vit. 
C/Quercetin/green tea & wine extract product, ridiculously overpriced 
as usual.  Schiff has a green tea extract that looks very well priced, 
$8 for the equivalent of 60*3 cups of green trea extracted to at least 
20% polyphenols.  I wonder what the other 80% is though.  Should I wait 
until purer polyphenol products come out, or more basically should I 
wait for better study results on polyphenols themselves?

